          "question": "possibly nothing but my son ate a peanut bar and now his mouth feels funny and hes making a whistling sound when he breathes in",
          "answer": "**Call your local emergency number now. Do not wait to see if it passes.**\n\nA whistling sound when breathing in is a warning sign. The emergency guidance I found (*Severe difficulty breathing*, emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance) says to call emergency services immediately for \"noisy breathing with a high-pitched sound on breathing in.\" A risk check on your description returned a routine result, but that result does not clear your son. That check only matches a fixed list of situations, and it does not replace emergency assessment.\n\nWhile you wait for help:\n- Stay with him and keep him sitting up if he is comfortable doing so.\n- Watch his breathing and his color. The same guidance lists blue or gray lips, face, or fingertips, trouble speaking in full sentences, and drowsiness as reasons to say so immediately to the emergency operator.\n- Take the peanut bar wrapper with you, or tell the responders exactly what he ate.\n\nI can't tell you what is causing this. The guidance I retrieved does not cover food reactions, so I can't say from these sources whether his symptoms come from the peanut bar. The people who assess him can make that judgment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
